Here, I'll explain the demonstration that my teammates and I presented at ELIXIR-2026, held at NMAM Institute of Technology (NMAMIT), Nitte.

The demonstration covered Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) and Sinusoidal Pulse Width Modulation (SPWM) using the Microchip dsPIC33 Digital Signal Controller.

Our goal wasn't just to make a motor run — we wanted to show why different modulation techniques matter and how they affect the performance of a three-phase induction motor.

🤔 First of all... why do we even need PWM?

This was probably the first question that came to mind when I started learning power electronics.

Imagine you have a DC supply of around 310V after rectification. If you connect that directly to a three-phase motor, it simply won't work — the motor needs three-phase AC, not DC.

So the question becomes: how do we convert DC into AC?

That's where an inverter comes in. But the inverter doesn't magically create AC — it simply turns its switches ON and OFF thousands of times every second.

Who tells those switches when to turn ON and OFF? That's exactly what the dsPIC33 does — it generates the gate pulses that control the inverter switches.

⚡ So what exactly is PWM?

PWM stands for Pulse Width Modulation.

Instead of changing the voltage directly, we change how long each pulse stays ON:

If a switch stays ON for a longer time, the average voltage increases.
If it stays ON for a shorter time, the average voltage decreases.

That's the basic idea behind PWM. It may look like we're only generating square pulses, but those pulses are actually controlling how much power reaches the motor.

🤨 But then... why doesn't PWM produce a perfect sine wave?

This is something I found really interesting.

The inverter output is basically switching between ON and OFF very rapidly. Because of these sudden transitions, the output waveform is no longer a smooth sine wave.

Whenever a waveform has sharp edges, it contains additional frequency components — these unwanted frequencies are called harmonics. You can think of harmonics as extra "noise" mixed in with the original 50 Hz signal.

The motor doesn't want those frequencies — it only wants a clean 50 Hz sine wave.

😕 Why are harmonics a problem?

When harmonics are present, the motor still runs — but not as efficiently. Some of the effects include:

The motor becomes hotter due to extra losses.
Torque becomes slightly uneven, causing vibration.
You may hear more humming or noise.
Efficiency decreases.
Electromagnetic interference (EMI) increases.

So yes, the motor works, but it isn't operating under the best conditions.

💡 So how can we improve it?

This is where SPWM comes into the picture.

SPWM stands for Sinusoidal Pulse Width Modulation. Instead of keeping every pulse the same width, we continuously vary the pulse width according to a sine wave — the pulses become narrow, then wider, then widest, and then narrow again.

When you observe the average value of all those pulses, it closely follows a sine wave. That's why SPWM gives much better output quality than standard PWM.

💻 Why did we use the dsPIC33?

For this demonstration, we used the Microchip dsPIC33 Digital Signal Controller because it's specifically designed for applications like motor control and power electronics.

It has built-in PWM modules, fast timers, ADC support, complementary PWM outputs, and dead-time generation — making it a great choice for controlling a three-phase inverter in real time.

